{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And he does not know, and he bears his guilt.</b> This passage concerns one into whose hand a doubtful case of karet has come, and he does not know whether he transgressed it or not — for example, there was before him forbidden fat and permissible fat, and he thought both were permitted and ate one of them; they said to him, 'One of the pieces was forbidden fat,' and he does not know whether he ate of the forbidden fat or of the permissible fat — this one brings a suspensive guilt-offering (asham talui), and it protects him as long as it has not become known to him that he certainly sinned; but if it becomes known to him, he brings a sin-offering. Or, for example, a woman in bed — doubt whether she is his wife or his sister. And the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded in the chapter 'Harini Nazir': 'And he does not know, and he bears his guilt' — over this matter shall sigh all the sighers." "<b>And one should ask:</b> for in the passage above, when his sin was made known to him, Scripture obligated him to bring an offering and called that offering a sin-offering (חטאת); whereas here, where he sinned unwittingly as well and his sin was not made known to him, Scripture does obligate him to bring an offering yet calls it a guilt-offering (אשם). Now know that the word חטאת is from the root of שגגה (error), and the word אשם is from the root of שממה (desolation), to indicate that one who sins has sinned in a matter in which he deserves to become desolate; if so, how does Scripture deal more stringently with one who has not been informed than with one who has been informed — calling the offering of one who has been informed a חטאת and the offering of one who has not been informed an אשם — and furthermore, the offering of one who has been informed is a goat, while the offering of one who has not been informed is a ram, whose value is greater?" "<b>But</b> the reason for this is that regarding a sin that is known to him, he is anxious and distressed about it, and the anxiety over the sin is an altar of atonement and one species among the species of repentance, as it is written (Psalms 38): ״כי עוני אגיד אדאג מחטאתי״; but this one who sinned and it was not known to him — he was neither anxious nor distressed, nor did he confess it. Therefore it is fitting that the Torah deal more stringently with him. And from this the sinner is called אשם, as it is written: ״ולא ידע ואשם״, and his offering is called אשם, as it is written: ״אשם הוא אשום אשם לה׳״ — that is, 'an אשם is the offering of this one; אשום אשם' — when his sin is not known and he did not become anxious and was not distressed over it — 'לה׳', to the One who knows hidden things. And since he has no knowledge of his sin except to HaShem alone, therefore it says concerning this: אשום אשם לה׳; like what they expounded regarding the goat of the new moon, of which it is said (Numbers 28): לחטאת לה׳ — a sin-offering that no one recognizes but HaShem." "<b>In the Midrash:</b> ״And he does not know, and he bears his guilt״ — R. Yosi the Galilean says: See, Scripture punished one who did not know; all the more so will He punish one who did know. R. Yosi says: If you wish to know the reward of the righteous, go out and learn from the first man, who was commanded only regarding a negative commandment and transgressed it — see how many deaths were decreed upon him and upon his generations. And which measure is greater, the measure of good or the measure of punishment? You must say: the measure of good. If the measure of punishment, which is the lesser, see how many deaths were decreed upon him and upon his generations — the measure of good, which is greater, how much the more so will it accrue to him who refrains from piggul and notar and who fasts on the Day of Atonements, for himself and for his generations and for the generations of his generations to the end of all generations. R. Akiva says: See, it says (Deuteronomy 17) ״על פי שנים עדים או שלשה עדים יקום דבר״ — if testimony is established by two witnesses, why is it stated 'three'? Rather, to bring the third one and deal stringently with him as though he had accomplished something, and to render his case liable for punishment and for conspiring testimony just as though the testimony were established through him. If so — Scripture punished one who merely attaches himself to transgressors as though he himself transgressed; all the more so will He grant reward to one who attaches himself to those who perform commandments as though he performed the commandment. R. Elazar ben Azaria says: See, it says (Deuteronomy 24) ״כי תקצור קצירך בשדך ושכחת עומר בשדה לא תשוב לקחתו וגו׳ למען יברכך וגו׳״ — Scripture fixed a blessing for one through whose agency a commandment comes without his knowledge. Say henceforth: a selah was tied in his garment and fell from him, and a poor man found it and was sustained by it — see, Scripture fixes for him a blessing."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:23:21.425647049Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}